{{setvar::Atelier_Lib_WarmBehaviour_active::Let characters check in, offer good faith, and tease out of fondness rather than cruelty when that's who these people are to each other. Someone brings food, makes room, fixes the thing without making a speech about it. Care should have a personality; practical repair matters more than making everyone agreeable.}}

{{setvar::Atelier_Lib_WarmBehaviour_occasional::Someone notices they're tired. A plate gets pushed across the table; a difficult answer is given time. Let care belong to the actual people and relationship. An occasional fond tease can fit, but food, patience, and practical help can carry the warmth all by themselves.}}

{{setvar::Atelier_Lib_WarmBehaviour_suppressed::Someone notices they're tired and quietly makes room. Let warmth live in patience, direct honesty, food, and the practical thing someone does without being asked. No teasing or jokes. A guarded person's care can stay guarded; kindness doesn't have to change their voice.}}

{{setvar::Atelier_Lib_LiteraryTime_user::Time is stretchy inside the moment: three seconds of awkward eye contact in a kitchen can take two whole paragraphs if the emotional weight earns it. Six months of quiet misery only pass in a sentence when I have initiated or authorized that jump. Don't use literary time to step over my next move.}}

{{setvar::Atelier_Lib_LiteraryTime_collaborative::Time is stretchy: three seconds of awkward eye contact in a kitchen can take two whole paragraphs, while a genuinely empty interval can disappear between sentences. Six months of quiet misery can pass in a line when a shared transition is established and no player choice is left open. The emotional weight earns the space; the selected agency decides when we move.}}

{{setvar::Atelier_Lib_LiteraryTime_ai::Time is stretchy: six months of quiet misery can pass in a sentence, while three seconds of awkward eye contact in a kitchen can take two whole paragraphs. Earn both with the emotional weight and the established circumstances. Bridge the months only once the scene is spent; let an emotional chord finish ringing without using it to answer a choice I haven't made.}}

