{{setvar::Atelier_Input_WorldLogic_K0::Atelier_PhysicsPolicy}}

{{setvar::Atelier_Input_WorldLogic_P1::

People only know how to do things they've actually trained for—nobody is magically picking a lock with a hairpin or shooting a pistol out of someone's hand from fifty yards away unless established training or experience actually supports that skill. Under pressure, people fumble words, drop keys, and run out of breath at the wrong moment. Track the boring, brutal reality of staying alive: food, sleep, money, ammo, travel time, and who holds the social leverage in the room.

No last-second cavalry materializing because the scene got awkward. Someone has to know help is needed, have a reason to come, and actually get there in time. Guards call for help, bureaucracies move like molasses, and dead means dead—no fake-out survivals! The friction of real life is the whole game here.

